This directory contains snapshots of:
=====================================

redis: https://github.com/antirez/redis.git branch 2.6
$ git describe --dirty --always
2.6.0-rc6-1-g013189e

redis-py: https://github.com/andymccurdy/redis-py.git branch master
$ git describe --dirty --always
fd82c5d

Directory layout:
=================

'redis/redis': server and hiredis C API source
'redis/redis-py': Python bindings
'examples/c' and 'examples/python': linuxcnc-specific redis examples.

later:
'include' eventually to contain a C include file describing the namespace and query templates.
'python' : any linuxcnc-specific redis-dependent Pyhton modules

LinuxCNC startup and shutdown:
=============================

ini options: you dont need any of them, but these are understood:

    [REDIS]
    SERVER_PATH=redis-server
    SERVER_LOG=/tmp/redis.log
    SERVER_ARGS=
    #DISABLE_SERVER=1

by default, the redis-server startup command is 'redis-server >/dev/null' .

--

post-build test to increase confidence (this runs quite a while so I dont include it in runtests):

$ cd src/redis/redis
$ make tests


Programming notes:
------------------

including config.h #defines or #undefs CONFIG_REDIS
for now, this defaults to true

for client configuration options, see redisparams.ini in this directory


Usage example - first steps:
============================

build. I assume all shells mentioned below have sourced scripts/rip-environment.

start the redis server in a separate window:
$ redis-server

new window - run Python sync subscriber demo:
$ cd src/redis
$ python subscribe-test.py

new window - run redis-cli and try a basic set/publish:
$ redis-cli
redis 127.0.0.1:6379> set param:5650 3.14
OK
redis 127.0.0.1:6379> publish params param:5650
(integer) 1

The publish command should trigger subscriber-test.py to retrieve the new value.

Now the whole thing from the interpreter - new window:

$ cd src/redis
$ rs274 -g -i redisparams.ini
executing
    1 N..... USE_LENGTH_UNITS(CANON_UNITS_MM)
    2 N..... SET_G5X_OFFSET(1, 0.0000, 0.0000, 0.0000, 0.0000, 0.0000, 0.0000)
    3 N..... SET_G92_OFFSET(0.0000, 0.0000, 0.0000, 0.0000, 0.0000, 0.0000)
    4 N..... SET_XY_ROTATION(0.0000)
    5 N..... SET_FEED_REFERENCE(CANON_XYZ)
READ => (debug,#5650)
retrieved param[5650] from redis: 3.14

    6 N..... MESSAGE("3.140000")
READ => #5650 = [3*94]
storing param[5650] = 282 in redis
notified 1 subscriber(s) in params channel
READ => 

the last command should have triggered subscriber-test.py, too.

Using the whole thing with an Axis config:
==========================================

With the above steps, you had redis-server running because you started
it explicitly. Kill redis-server now, since linuxcnc starts redis-server
automatically. 

then:

$ cd configs/sim/axis
$ linuxcnc axis_mm_redis.ini

this starts axis and redis-server in the background, with redis params in the interp enabled
all the pub/sub examples above with redis-cli and subscribe-test.py should work as above.

Also, this configuration enables persistent parameters through redis, instead of the emc.var file.

To get emc.var files into redis, see src/redis/emcvarload.sh .
To export the redis param hash into an emc.var file, see src/redis/emcvardump.py.

Param range 5610-5710 is the 'UI commincation param range'. See interp_internal.hh.

Parameters in the range 30-5399 are considered persistent when inserted into redis.

To make param 4711 persisent, and subject to notification via redis on update, set the key like so:

redis-cli> hset param 4711 0

redis-server, or eg Axis started by 'linuxcnc' must be running.

An update to param 4711 will only be subject to notification after next linuxcnc startup.


Interpreter Programming notes:
------------------------------

The basic functionality for numbered parameters through redis, and persistence is in place.
interp_params.cc still needs polishing; after more redis integration it will become clearer  
which redis interactions are needed.

